# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Showcase Engine — environment variables
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development, or set them in the client's Vercel
# project (Settings → Environments → Production) for a deployment.
#
# `.env.local` is read ONCE at server startup. Adding a variable does nothing
# until you restart `npm run dev` — which looks exactly like a broken integration.
#
# Full guide: docs/BUILDING-A-SITE.md §4d (delivery) and §6a (deploying).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Which site this deployment is ────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which client config this deployment renders at the root route "/".
# Must match a config registered in /configs/index.ts (the file's `client` slug).
#
# On the shared `main` branch this can point at any demo for local preview.
# On a client branch (client/[name]) set this to that client's slug so their
# Vercel project always renders their site at "/".
#
# NOTE: a client branch registers exactly ONE config and the engine falls back
# to it, so this is optional there — but set it anyway, so a mistake surfaces as
# a clear error instead of as the wrong site. Naming a slug that ISN'T
# registered throws at startup, which is why it must not be set on a Vercel
# project until that project's production branch points at the client branch.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ACTIVE_CLIENT=your-client-slug

# Canonical origin, used for absolute URLs in metadata, JSON-LD and the sitemap.
# Resolution order: config `business.url` → this → Vercel's own
# VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL (set automatically). Set this when the client has
# a real domain and it isn't already in the config.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://acme-plumbing.com

# ── Contact-form delivery ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A channel activates by the PRESENCE of its variables — nothing in the config
# holds a secret. Enable as many as you like; delivery succeeds if any accepts.
# `contact.delivery.channels` in the config may narrow which ones are attempted.
#
# With NO channel configured: logs to the server console in development (so the
# form is testable with zero accounts) and reports an error to the visitor in
# production — never a silent drop.

# --- email (Resend) ---
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# The verified sender. `onboarding@resend.dev` works with NO domain setup, but
# only delivers to the Resend account holder's own address — enough to prove the
# wiring before a client's DNS is ready.
# RESEND_FROM="Acme Plumbing <hello@acme-plumbing.com>"
# Optional per-deployment recipient override. Beats config `delivery.email.to`,
# which in turn beats `business.email`.
# CONTACT_EMAIL_TO=owner@acme-plumbing.com

# --- telegram ---
# From @BotFather. A bot CANNOT start a conversation: the owner must message it
# (or the bot must be added to a group and someone posts there) before a chat
# exists at all.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456789:AAxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# Run `npm run telegram:chatid -- --write` to find and write this. Prefer a
# GROUP for a client (its id is negative) so enquiries reach several staff.
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=123456789
